course: biology
lesson: 172
title: Coral reef builders: polyps, bleaching, sand from parrotfish and giant clams
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A coral feels like stone, but it is a city of tiny animals called polyps, each like a little sea anemone, and each builds a cup of limestone around itself; millions of cups stacked over thousands of years make a reef.
explain: Inside the polyps live tiny algae that make food from sunlight and share it, which is why reefs need clear, shallow, sunny water, and why a reef in poor blue water is full of life, like an oasis in a desert.
explain: If the water gets too hot, the corals push their algae out and turn white like bone; this is bleaching, and if the heat lasts too long the coral starves.
explain: Walk on a white tropical beach and much of the sand under your feet may have passed through a parrotfish, which crunches coral with its beak and poops out fine sand.
fact: Corals are animals, not plants or rocks: colonies of tiny polyps that build hard skeletons of limestone and at night stretch out tentacles to catch tiny food.
fact: Coral reefs grow in warm, clear, shallow water, where the tiny algae living inside the polyps get enough sunlight to make food.
fact: When water gets too warm, corals push out their algae and turn white, which is called bleaching; big bleaching events have hit the Great Barrier Reef many times since 1998.
fact: Parrotfish bite algae off coral with beak-like teeth and grind up bits of coral; one big parrotfish can make hundreds of kilos of sand a year.
fact: Some parrotfish make a see-through bubble of slime to sleep in at night, and they make a new bubble every night.
fact: The giant clam is the biggest clam in the world and can weigh more than two hundred kilos; the story of giant clams trapping divers' legs is a myth, because they close slowly and usually not fully.
fact: On a few nights a year many corals release tiny pink bundles of eggs and sperm into the water at the same time, like an underwater snowstorm.
fact: The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is the largest coral reef system on Earth, made of thousands of separate reefs.
fact: People say the Great Barrier Reef is the only living thing you can see from space, but it is made of countless living things, and astronauts can see cities and roads too.
fact: Crown-of-thorns starfish eat living coral and sometimes come in huge numbers; giant triton snails eat them, and divers remove them to help reefs.
fact: Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor but are home to a huge share of sea life, and they protect coasts from waves like natural walls.
fact: Not all corals live in warm water: some reefs grow in cold, dark water deep down, for example off Norway, and some are thousands of years old.
exercise: What is a coral? | A colony of tiny animals called polyps | A kind of sea plant with roots in the sand below | A rock that grows by itself | A type of seaweed
exercise: What do coral polyps build their skeletons from? | Limestone | Wood | Ice | Sand glued with slime
exercise: When do coral polyps stretch out their tentacles to catch food? | At night | Only at noon | Never, they do not eat | Only in winter
exercise: In what kind of water do most coral reefs grow? | Warm, clear and shallow water | Cold, muddy rivers | Deep water with no light at all | Fresh water in lakes
exercise: What is coral bleaching? | Corals push out their algae in hot water and turn white | Corals are painted white by divers | Corals grow white flowers in spring to attract fish | Corals turn white when they are healthy and happy in the sun
exercise: How does a parrotfish make sand? | It grinds up coral and poops out sand | It digs sand up from the beach with its fins | It spits out shells | It crushes rocks with its strong tail
exercise: How much sand can one big parrotfish make in a year? | Hundreds of kilos | A teaspoon | One grain | Exactly one bucket
exercise: Where do some parrotfish sleep? | In a see-through bubble of slime | In empty glass bottles lying on the sea floor | On the beach | Inside giant clams
exercise: How heavy can a giant clam be? | More than two hundred kilos | About as heavy as an apple | About one kilo | Ten tonnes
exercise: Is it true that giant clams trap divers' legs? | No, they close slowly and usually not fully | Yes, they snap shut in a second on anything that touches them | Yes, they hunt divers on purpose | No, giant clams have no shell
exercise: What happens when corals spawn? | Many corals release tiny pink bundles at the same time | Corals pull up their roots and walk to a new place on the reef | Corals change into fish | Corals lose all their colour
exercise: What is the Great Barrier Reef? | The largest coral reef system on Earth | A wall built by people to stop sharks | One single giant coral | A reef in the Arctic
exercise: Why is it wrong to call the Great Barrier Reef the only living thing seen from space? | It is made of countless living things, and cities can be seen too | It cannot be seen from a plane | It is not alive at all, it is just old rock and sand under the water | It is too small to see from anywhere
exercise: What does a crown-of-thorns starfish eat? | Living coral | Sea grass | Plastic | Only dead fish
exercise: Which animal eats crown-of-thorns starfish? | The giant triton snail | The clownfish | The dugong, which swims slowly over the reef | The sea horse
exercise: How much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover? | Less than one percent | About half of it | All of it, from one side to the other | About a quarter
exercise: Where was a cold-water coral reef found deep down? | Off Norway | In the Sahara | In a swimming pool | On a mountain top
test: «diver here, touched a coral, feels like a rock so it's a rock right» What is right? | No, it is a colony of tiny animals with limestone skeletons | Yes, corals are just rocks | Yes, rocks that grow flowers | No, it is a plant like a tree, with roots, a trunk and branches of stone
test: In SpongeBob, Patrick the starfish lives happily on a reef. Which real starfish is a danger to coral reefs? | The crown-of-thorns starfish, which eats living coral | Every kind of starfish eats coral, all the time | Starfish never eat anything | Starfish eat only sponges, like SpongeBob's friend Patrick does
test: What would happen if the sea around a reef stayed too hot for many weeks? | The corals would bleach and might starve | The corals would grow faster than ever | The reef would turn into sand at once | Nothing, corals love very hot water
test: «my uncle says giant clams snap shut and drown divers, legit?» What is right? | No, that is a myth: they close slowly and usually not fully | Yes, it happens every week, ask any diver on the reef | Yes, they bite like sharks | No, giant clams cannot close at all
test: «sat on a white beach in the Maldives, someone said it's fish poo lol» Could that be right? | Yes, much of the sand there comes from parrotfish grinding up coral | No, all sand comes from mountains, washed down by rivers to the sea | No, sand is made from salt | Yes, but only from whale poo
test: Why would a reef not grow in deep, dark water off a tropical island? | The algae inside the polyps need sunlight to make food | The water there is too warm | Fish eat all the corals there, so none are left to grow | Corals hate salt water
test: «3 parrotfish asleep on the reef, each makes a new bubble every night. how many bubbles over 2 nights» | 6 | 3 | 2 | 5
test: A diver sees the sea turn pink with tiny floating bundles one night after a full moon. What is happening? | The corals are spawning all at once | The reef is bleaching | A ship spilled paint | Fish are laying their eggs all over the sand
test: «reefs are tiny, who cares if they die» What is a good answer? | They cover under one percent of the sea floor but hold a huge share of sea life | They are huge and cover the whole ocean, from coast to coast | Nothing lives on reefs anyway | Reefs only matter to tourists who come to dive
test: «corals only live in hot seas, everyone knows that» What is right? | No, some reefs grow in cold, dark water deep down, for example off Norway | Yes, always | Yes, only in the Red Sea | No, they only live in rivers
test: Divers pull hundreds of crown-of-thorns starfish off a reef. Why? | The starfish come in huge numbers and eat the living coral | They want to eat them for dinner | Starfish are endangered and need saving by divers | The starfish block out the sunlight that the reef fish need to see
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the biological nature of a coral? | It is a colony of minute animals, termed polyps, which secrete skeletons of limestone | It is a mineral formation devoid of life | It is a species of marine plant | It is a single giant organism of the seaweed family
exam: Identify the principal cause of the phenomenon designated coral bleaching. | The expulsion of symbiotic algae consequent upon excessive water temperature | The deposition of salt crystals upon the reef | The consumption of coral by parrotfish | An excess of sunlight in water of moderate temperature during the winter months
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the giant clam. | The assertion that it entraps the limbs of divers is unfounded, since it closes slowly and incompletely | It is known to seize and drown divers with regularity | It possesses no shell whatsoever | It weighs not more than one kilogram
exam: Ascertain the origin of a considerable proportion of the white sand upon certain tropical beaches. | Fragments of coral ground and excreted by parrotfish | Particles of salt deposited by evaporation of the sea | Pulverised volcanic glass of black colour | Shells of the giant clam crushed by waves
